<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khafre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">26th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> · Also known as Chephren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khafre was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Fourth Dynasty in the 26th century BCE and a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>. He built the second pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, and the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> is generally attributed to his reign. His diorite seated statue from his valley temple is one of the masterpieces of Old Kingdom sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khafre.jpg" alt="Khafre" width="320" height="613" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José-Manuel Benito · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khafra_-_Quefr%C3%A9n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khafre was the brother and successor of King Djedefre. Some scholars think his mother was Queen Meritites I, because an inscription says she was honoured under Khafre; others argue that it shows only that she died during his reign, and he may instead have been a son of Queen Henutsen. He had several wives and at least twelve sons and three or four daughters. Queen Meresankh III, a niece of Khafre, was the mother of Nebemakhet, Duaenre, Niuserre, Khentetka and Shepsetkau. Hekenuhedjet bore Sekhemkare, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menkaure">Menkaure</a> and Khamerernebty II may have been children of Queen Khamerernebty I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>There is no agreement on the date of his reign. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a> leaves its length blank and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> gives 66 years, but most scholars place it between 24 and 26 years. They rely on the will of Prince Nekure, a son of Khafre, which is dated to the Year of the 12th Count, and on a painted date, the Year of the 13th occurrence, on a casing stone of mastaba G 7650.</p><p>Khafre built the second-largest pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, called Wer(en)-Khafre, meaning Khafre is Great. A small subsidiary pyramid beside it holds a burial whose owner is not clear. His valley temple stood close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, next to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> temple, and inscriptions at its entrance mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a>. The Great Sphinx of Giza is said to date to his time, because it lies close to his temple complex and resembles his statues in the face. It may have been carved as a guardian of his pyramid and a symbol of royal power, and it was deified in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p><p>More statues survive of Khafre than of any other Old Kingdom ruler, almost all from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. A hall of the valley temple has 23 depressions in the floor where life-size statues stood, and one is wider than the rest. It has been suggested that the 24 statues related to the hours of the day. Auguste Mariette found nine of them in 1860, with fragments of a tenth in a pit, and they are now in the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p>Greek writers described him differently. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> called him Sûphis II, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and Diodorus, writing more than 2,000 years later, portrayed him as a tyrant who ruled for 56 years and was followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menkaure">Menkaure</a>, who restored peace and piety.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khafre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…found in the palace, as were fragments of Egyptian stone vessels inscribed with the names of the pharaohs <b>Khafre</b> and Pepi I. The tablets were written in the Sumerian cuneiform script, used both for Sumerian words and for the local Semitic language, Eblaite, which is usually classed with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Fourth Dynasty (2613–2494 BCE) was the zenith of royal power: Sneferu, then his son Khufu (2589–2566 BCE), whose sons Djedefre and <b>Khafre</b> may have quarrelled, then Menkaure, Shepseskaf and perhaps Djedefptah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
